00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news  on today's programme concerns the uk's  climate credentials as the hosts of cop  26 could be undermined  as mps support a cut to the foreign aid  budget  could natural gas be used to create  clean energy  we find out how a net zero emission  power station  might work and the new edition will soon  be  beavering away to help flood management  on exmoor  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and we start with a decision  by mps to support a reduction in  overseas aid spending  from 0.7 of gross national income to 0.5  the government says spending on climate  initiatives is protected  but with the uk hosting the crucial cop  26 summit in november  a whitehall source told sky news the  summit president alok sharma  is concerned a failure to honour the  commitment will make it harder to  persuade developing countries to sign up  to emissions targets  here's what conservative mp tim lauten  has to say in the commons  global britain is about projecting not  just military and diplomatic influence  not just about pursuing new trading  and investment partnerships beyond this  continent but complementary to this is  the exercise  of soft power climate change is a major  focus of it we're  chairing cop for goodness sake what  message does that send  to the rest of the world this is a force  economy at the wrong time  well our correspondent katerina vatosi  is in bangladesh where alex sharma  visited recently to discuss the issue of  climate finance  and katarina what role does uk aid play  in a country like bangladesh which is so  vulnerable to climate change well a lot  of the aid money which the uk government  has spent in bangladesh over the past  decade or so  has been involved in projects that have  touched people dealing with the very  immediate threat of climate change here  in bangladesh we spoke  with the director of one of not only  bangladesh's but the world's  leading and largest ngos it's called  brac and over the past decade there's  been 450 million pounds worth of uk  government money which has gone in  to financing many of their projects and  a lot of those  are to do with climate change helping  coastal communities  deal with some of the worst weather  situations  that have got worse progressively  because of climate vulnerabilities  over the past 10 years and the director  of that organization told us that the  the cut in the uk foreign aid budget was  like a gut  punch it would have a dramatic impact on  the people that had seen  the benefits of some of those programs  over the past 10 years but also really  alok sharma when he was here said that  climate financing was such a totemic  issue with regards to getting vulnerable  countries on board  heading into cop 26 so this will be seen  as another significant  damage to that reputation katarina thank  you  in today's other climate news the uk's  first marine park is one of five  projects getting a share of 50  million pounds from the national lottery  heritage fund  plymouth sound has received nearly 10  million pounds to help protect wildlife  and explore how  seagrass can be used to help the  environment grants have also been  awarded to a major experiment  in wet farming and a project to preserve  the cairngorms landscape  climate change could be treated as an  emergency in the same way as coronavirus  was according to researchers  glasgow caledonian university center for  climate justice is calling for  developed countries to give more money  and technology  to poorer nations to help them adapt  they also want governments to  share data with the public on the deaths  and damage caused by  extreme weather events the un has  published a new draft of a paris-style  agreement on biodiversity with  21 proposals to protect nature it sets  out targets to reduce  pesticide use eliminate plastic  pollution and put at least  a third of the planet under conservation  protection by 2030  the agreement will be negotiated and  signed at the next global  biodiversity conference in october  the environment agency says water  companies must do more to reduce  serious pollution incidents its latest  annual report found  none of the nine english water and  sewerage companies  achieved all of the environmental  expectations set out for them  for 2015-2020 southern water and  southwest water were the worst  performing companies  for cutting pollution last year and the  welsh government is encouraging people  to plant  more trees to help whales reach net zero  by 2050.  mps are working with farmers and  landowners to identify areas where new  woodlands could be created  it's estimated around 86 million trees  will need to be planted over the next  nine years  if wales wants to achieve its emissions  targets  to the united states now where after the  warmest june on record parts of the  country are experiencing  yet more extreme heat a weather  phenomenon  called a heat dome has developed over  the southwest with  temperatures climbing well above average  during the day and then not falling much  overnight  the intense heat has seen death valley  register what could be confirmed  as the highest ever recorded temperature  on earth  of 54.4 degrees celsius  well the severity of the hot weather has  reinforced the dangers of human-induced  climate change with scientists saying  that global warming  is making the situation worse well our  correspondent greg milam is in palm  springs in california for us now so greg  there are experts who've expressed  concern that the american west is stuck  in this cycle of heat  and drought and fire is there a plan to  try and break that cycle  well they're trying their best and it  certainly feels like that cycle now is  is somewhat inevitable uh the scientists  say these heat waves are getting more  frequent  they're getting more intense so what do  you do about that we know cities are  looking at ways you can adapt  designing a landscape to provide more  shade for example  even talking about whether people need  to work at night rather than during the  day  shifting that whole way we we live our  lives because it's more bearable at  night although  here actually at night the record  temperatures still the minimum  temperatures are  hitting records one meteorologist here  has said to us  in the last couple of days that he can  see a a situation in the next couple of  decades where a place like this would be  uninhabitable  simply because those temperatures are so  hot uh during the summer and of course  they're used to it here we're in the  desert  it's still extreme for them here but  there are tens of millions of americans  who are experiencing these heat waves  who don't  uh usually and that is what is unusual  uh this year  uh and on top of that of course we have  the fires the drought the wildfires that  are more intense  earlier in the year and spreading much  more quickly earlier in the year  again something that's very different  from from what's happened the heat  actually is easing over the next couple  of days but the question is after the  last few weeks  that a lot of people have here is simply  when is the next one coming  greg thank you the uk's first  net zero emissions power plant could be  built on t  side if plans put forward by a group of  energy companies  gets the go-ahead those involved claim  that it would mean natural gas could be  used to create  clean and efficient low-cost electricity  so how would it work well traditional  power plants generate electricity by  burning natural gas  as their fuel they use a turbine natural  gas is added  along with a stream of air which  combusts and expands through this  turbine  causing a generator to spin a magnet  making electricity  and because air is made up of nitrogen  oxygen carbon dioxide and several other  chemical elements the emissions left  over after the energy creation process  are a blend of all of those chemicals  now nearly all combustion byproducts  have negative effects on the environment  and on our health one way to try to  reduce the harmful emissions is to  capture and store them  but to do that you need complex and  expensive technology to separate out all  of those chemicals  the proposed development on t side aims  to simplify  that process chief executive of eight  rivers cam hosey  told us how fitbit cycle  it combusts the natural gas fuel with  pure oxygen  as opposed to ordinary air and  as a result the combustion product that  comes through through this system  is pure co2 and water which is used to  drive the turbine  and to generate the electricity because  the  system itself only produces co2 in water  the separation process to get to the  pure co2  is relatively easy and inherent in the  technology itself  as a result we end up with a pure co2  product which can then be  taken away and sequestered underground  but would this process really result in  fewer emissions and is that the same as  net zero  well joining me now is dr mai bowie a  specialist in carbon capture and senior  research associate at the center for  environmental policy at imperial college  london  uh welcome to you do you think this  could be a game changer a way of  reducing our emissions  uh yes indeed so carbon capture and  storage is  well recognized as a fairly essential  technology  in our transition towards net zero  when you're looking at different sectors  so for example in the power sector  you need these types of ccs technologies  to kind of provide dispatchable reliable  power to help balance the intimacy of  renewables  um in you know the industrial sector  there's not many other options so you  need  carbon capture and storage technologies  to help reduce emissions there so  overall you know in across different  sectors you've got the need for ccs  and also for co2 removal to kind of get  negative  emissions to kind of offset other  emissions where we can't abate  co2 and presumably the technologies are  different depending on which sector  you're talking about  how advanced are we in those  technologies  here in the uk yes so  normally there's a portfolio of  different technologies and they have  different levels of technology readiness  level or trl  and the good news is that we do have  technologies at that higher trl level  and  ready to kind of deploy today so if we  wanted to do  carbon capture and storage it is very  much uh possible to do  um just using these sort of higher more  advanced  developed technologies and and in the  future  how big a role do you think that power  plants with carbon capture  will play in the race to net zero or do  you think the main focus will remain  on renewables so i think it's a  combination of all the different  technologies  um and you know it's better to kind of  minimize the risk in our transition to  try to use a portfolio  of technologies and diversify and so the  issue with renewables is the  intermittency  because you know if the sun's not  shining or the wind's not blowing we  don't have energy and  you can still have batteries energy but  it's slightly  um you know maybe not quite there yet on  in terms of the large scale  capacities that we may need and also in  terms of the cost so having ccs as sort  of the backup  will definitely be needed dr maybe  thanks very much indeed  and finally the national trust says the  first baby beaver to be born on  exmor for 400 years has been captured on  camera  the baby known as a kit was filmed at  the charities holnercut estate in  somerset  where the animals were reintroduced last  year the beavers help with natural flood  management  and improve biodiversity on the estate  well that's everything from us for today  on tomorrow's show sky sports news hits  the road in an electric car  visiting sports venues across the south  of england on a mission to go green  thanks for watching see you then

